From b03b382bb0f30846233f470f2bd912f40297eacf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:37:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Change the narrow crossing from 15 to 30 degrees

C.ANGLES is now [0 30 45 60]. The column headers in the walkthrough followed
the old list literally, so they are now built from C.ANGLES rather than
hardcoded -- changing the angle in one place changes it everywhere.

This does NOT fix the degenerate condition, and the docs now say so rather
than implying otherwise. Measured on the noise-free ground truth, with no
fitting and no noise: at KAPPA = 16 a 30 degree crossing returns a SINGLE
lobe at Lmax 4, 6 and 8, exactly as 15 degrees did. Two Watson populations
that close merge before any deconvolution is involved.

Where the boundary actually sits, measured at Lmax 8 over KAPPA 8 to 128:

  kappa   dispersion   30deg  45deg  60deg
      8      22.4 deg      1      2      2
     16      14.9 deg      1      2      2
     32      10.3 deg      1      2      2
     48       8.4 deg      2      2      2
    128       5.1 deg      2      2      2

So 30 degrees needs KAPPA >= 48, about 8 degrees of dispersion, before it
separates at all; 45 and 60 separate everywhere. That note is now in
mc_config.m next to ANGLES, where anyone changing the angle will read it,
and in notebooks/README.md as one of the results that looks like a failure
and is not.

Raising KAPPA is not done here: mc_config.m picks 16 deliberately because
real white matter disperses and an estimated response absorbs that
dispersion. Trading it away to make one condition resolvable is a design
decision, not a fix.

